#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166448 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d155d72a-89b3-537e-a7da-d782215e53f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "df824804-901a-5c54-9004-d079ef613105" :question/math-academy-id "e-21423" :question/problem "$$\ny''' - 3y'' - 6y' + 8y = 2 + 5e^{-x}, \\qquad \\big\\{ y_1 = e^{-2x}, \\: y_2 = e^{x}, \\: y_3 = e^{4x} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)e^{-2x} +u_2(x)e^{x} + u_3(x)e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $u_1(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3},\n\\qquad\n{u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int{{\\frac{{g(x){W_i}(x)}}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x}}, \n\\qquad\ni=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 2 + 5e^{-x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= e^{ - 2x}, \\qquad y_2= e^{x}, \\qquad y_3= e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 16\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}[1(16-4)-1(-32-16)+1(-2-4)] \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(12+48-6) \\\\  & =54{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find $u_1,$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4{e}^{5x}-{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(2+5{e}^{-x})(3{e}^{5x})}{54{e}^{3x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int (2+5{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int (2{e}^{2x}+5{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}({e}^{2x}+5{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166449 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39917981-2d1c-5cf2-8c20-ff2acec4ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "68a1f42e-a8c7-5be3-b051-68bfd4707347" :question/math-academy-id "e-21429" :question/problem "$$\ny''' + 4y'  =  6,  \\qquad \\big\\{ y_1 = 1, \\: y_2 = \\cos(2x), \\: y_3 = \\sin(2x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x) = A + B \\cos(2x) + C \\sin(2x) + u_1(x) + u_2(x) \\cos(2x) + u_3(x) \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $u_3(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3},\n\\qquad\n{u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int \\frac{g(x){W_i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x, \n\\qquad\ni=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 6.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= 1, \\qquad y_2= \\cos(2x), \\qquad y_3= \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1,$ $y_2,$ and $y_3$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find $u_3,$ we compute the determinant $W_3(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39917981-2d1c-5cf2-8c20-ff2acec4ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2fe824c-59de-5f8c-bfc2-34c409ed9a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\cos (x) \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164008} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b4a7eb-fd0b-5705-8750-4c1eb45ea625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164009} {:answer/id #uuid "d0397eff-9845-5ca4-85d7-da8dff09bc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164010} {:answer/id #uuid "32048019-a512-5096-b5a6-812cb621abd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164011} {:answer/id #uuid "79a9bae6-94b7-553e-ab9f-1c73597bd67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2fe824c-59de-5f8c-bfc2-34c409ed9a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\cos (x) \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164008} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b904b3f-e2d9-5d5e-849f-c87c7c2b5d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "716b0900-6b2a-535c-9122-4199af0eaf40" :question/math-academy-id "q-327781" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{3}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d155d72a-89b3-537e-a7da-d782215e53f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b45869a2-eba2-5dfa-8a6b-54f03fa9879e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164959} {:answer/id #uuid "b514eb3f-730f-58a3-a37a-b8c3ab7f38ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164960} {:answer/id #uuid "eabf6c7d-146a-58a9-9e1a-65df692adba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164961} {:answer/id #uuid "675ae05e-6fd7-5a47-99ec-d127006ccf1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164962} {:answer/id #uuid "6b04bb8b-08d7-5c35-8a92-7214ca5bad0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b45869a2-eba2-5dfa-8a6b-54f03fa9879e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164959} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93ed0dd-52bc-5410-8524-5ef7db40d10a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d3e846-f48b-50eb-a3de-fa2dc394ba0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344659" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}-5{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+8y=6x{e}^{2x},\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{2x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & {e}^{2x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -{e}^{-x} & 2{e}^{2x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & 4{e}^{2x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}[1(32-16)-1(-16-4)+1(-4-2)] \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}(16-(-20)+(-6)) \\\\  & =30{e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & ={W}_{2}({y}_{1},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -{e}^{-x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4{e}^{3x}-(-{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(6x{e}^{2x})(5{e}^{3x})}{30{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d155d72a-89b3-537e-a7da-d782215e53f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6c1d18-cd30-54d8-9e9a-35ed95919e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372414} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc6cb45-4af1-54f7-851f-fe585c832712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372415} {:answer/id #uuid "507539f6-ced0-5462-85cf-a866848fa251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372416} {:answer/id #uuid "35bfcb0d-d558-5f82-8497-17a3c29540ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372417} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8c63dc-ce19-506b-93a1-0db0f44aeea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bfcb0d-d558-5f82-8497-17a3c29540ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7474362-1fb8-53a0-aa7c-df05daa0d20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a37232c1-6c3a-5f1a-805f-0b7ed15f6c0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344672" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=12{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{-2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{3x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){e}^{-2x}+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=12{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{3x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{3x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[1(9-3)-1(-18-12)+1(-2-4)] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(6+30-6) \\\\  & =30{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & ={W}_{2}({y}_{1},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{3x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & 3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(12{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(5{e}^{x})}{30{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d155d72a-89b3-537e-a7da-d782215e53f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c65e4598-a16b-5152-9b7c-6d3b015925a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡({e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/r