3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x} \\\\ 16{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 16 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}[1(4-2)-1(-16-32)+1(-4-16)] \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(2-(-48)+(-20)) \\\\  & =30{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & ={W}_{3}({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}-(-4{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}})(5{e}^{-3x})}{30{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d155d72a-89b3-537e-a7da-d782215e53f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f32410-e170-5711-a55f-cf2206e52504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372428} {:answer/id #uuid "a0776768-d434-5629-9f69-f02aed7592c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372429} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9fffa4-3dff-5aed-8e9c-d098f07e76a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372430} {:answer/id #uuid "21c098fd-f588-5d57-98b2-48dabd83f9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372431} {:answer/id #uuid "92516dcc-3e3b-5363-a879-8d616ec7668f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0776768-d434-5629-9f69-f02aed7592c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372429} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b538f7d-b771-5409-9db7-a532336de795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8152533f-1707-56fb-a329-e4602b3d4222" :question/math-academy-id "q-344686" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}-{y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x)\\cdot 1+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{-3x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & {e}^{-3x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ 0 & -3{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 0 & 9{e}^{-3x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 16\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1(-48-36)-1(0-0)+1(0-0)] \\\\  & =-84{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-(-3{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}})(7{e}^{x})}{(-84{e}^{x})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d155d72a-89b3-537e-a7da-d782215e53f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bcbddc1-8e96-5d73-a0a0-54e84425a30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372435} {:answer/id #uuid "96aa4d6c-4aca-54e2-bd1f-65a52cf8b7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372436} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5eda6f-653f-5bd8-9bad-9535251a68ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372437} {:answer/id #uuid "5593b169-8655-5b49-b6bf-5bfa24152629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372438} {:answer/id #uuid "e50536a1-6ba3-5544-9714-278b5732234b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bcbddc1-8e96-5d73-a0a0-54e84425a30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372435} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6965817-756d-5ea4-ae77-995919a6ec51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4373e0ad-5362-5a22-ab1b-2df7fe9b9fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-344688" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+6{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-30y=35{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{-5x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){e}^{-5x}+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{-3x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=35{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-5x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5x} & {e}^{-3x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ -5{e}^{-5x} & -3{e}^{-3x} & 2{e}^{2x} \\\\ 25{e}^{-5x} & 9{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-5x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{2x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 9 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}[1(-12-18)-1(-20-50)+1(-45+75)] \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}(-30-(-70)+30) \\\\  & =70{e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & ={W}_{3}({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5x} & {e}^{-3x} \\\\ -5{e}^{-5x} & -3{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-8x}-(-5{e}^{-8x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(35{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{-8x})}{70{e}^{-6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39917981-2d1c-5cf2-8c20-ff2acec4ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b59dbd-7c3a-5b2b-a9ce-b572453d446d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164971} {:answer/id #uuid "8798047c-49b3-53f1-bc69-a8e28675eb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164972} {:answer/id #uuid "d92bf7a2-e208-57f9-bc7d-99acd14b0240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164973} {:answer/id #uuid "e62351ab-86e1-5489-b11c-3f96a6970a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164974} {:answer/id #uuid "f422a773-2df3-5f9e-b5fc-8646a15697ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8798047c-49b3-53f1-bc69-a8e28675eb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164972} :answer-field/id #uuid "68aff912-1b8a-5a44-baea-83a5238b1936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0ccf32-6f43-56b1-ac5d-39e4b594e1be" :question/math-academy-id "q-344824" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39917981-2d1c-5cf2-8c20-ff2acec4ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b96be7dd-95e3-5d4f-a7dc-2e739f4ec7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372442} {:answer/id #uuid "004712d5-ef48-5084-9e11-a9ffc1a9f11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372443} {:answer/id #uuid "b56467ff-27b5-573e-b045-182e079cc2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372444} {:answer/id #uuid "aef1c957-641e-5257-a762-7c9c0d463747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372445} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca5d6ec-634c-5d8b-b64d-d2b7268982bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef1c957-641e-5257-a762-7c9c0d463747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372445} :answer-field/id #uuid "203852fa-8b83-51b8-ac61-213e4d8f2c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7679bb1e-afbf-586d-a4f5-9a1e8e8b870a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344828" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+9{y}^{′}=3{e}^{x},\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=3{e}^{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)-(-27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =27[{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)] \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3{e}^{x}\\cdot 3}{27}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39917981-2d1c-5cf2-8c20-ff2acec4ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f630d77-28b5-53b6-977b-7c80895e4646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372449} {:answer/id #uuid "74472024-daee-5530-ad5f-3d7237816c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372450} {:answer/id #uuid "0426c0be-3cbf-5999-ab00-d277730b7e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372451} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dfd51a-4daa-530c-8672-eacb512d9cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372452} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fd1cd1-b387-5c6c-9102-7953f4b85556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f630d77-28b5-53b6-977b-7c80895e4646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372449} :answer-field/id #uuid "75a8d2c2-4192-5383-8eb3-b885b9126918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91928537-86cb-513e-a054-9cb69dfec7ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-344832" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-8y=(8x+4){e}^{2x},\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{1}(x){e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=(8x+4){e}^{2x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2[{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)] \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(8x+4){e}^{2x}\\cdot 2}{16{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int (8x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39917981-2d1c-5cf2-8c20-ff2acec4ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af2c898-d49f-52fc-924b-1772ec42db06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372456} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa78662-be74-587d-9373-30fd6e6419de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372457} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba019a0-0d99-5f2a-b7b7-3c2b72d7d498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372458} {:answer/id #uuid "29c9c81a-0f0c-5e51-9751-05ba853309f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372459} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e1737e-99ed-52c9-b5d9-de6c0f151e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa78662-be74-587d-9373-30fd6e6419de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372457} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4246fb1-a946-564d-bbbe-74e0a2a36180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbaba68c-dad0-5dec-8363-3fc3af87a625" :question/math-academy-id "q-344837" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=-{sec}^{3}⁡(x),\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=-{sec}^{3}⁡(x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(-{sec}^{3}⁡(x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
